-- Passage rg4-character-setting-plot-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f878f823-20fc-5f6e-af95-b57b374548d0', 'rg4-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Waiting for Wings$q$, $q$Ms. Adeyemi's fourth-grade class kept five tiny caterpillars in a mesh cage by the window. They were painted lady caterpillars. When they arrived, each one was no longer than a grain of rice.

Theo Lindgren checked the cage every morning before he even took off his backpack. He wrote what he saw in a notebook. Day 3: "They are eating the leaves fast." Day 8: "They are as long as my thumb now."

On Day 10, the caterpillars climbed to the top of the cage. They hung upside down in the shape of the letter J. By the next morning, each one had turned into a hard brown case called a chrysalis.

Theo was worried. The chrysalises did not move at all. "Are they okay?" he asked. "Shouldn't we open one to check?"

Ms. Adeyemi shook her head. "Big changes are happening inside," she said. "If we open it, the butterfly cannot finish growing."

So Theo kept writing. Day 12: "Nothing." Day 14: "Still nothing." Day 16: "Still nothing, but I am being patient."

On Day 19, Theo walked in and gasped. An orange and black butterfly was resting on the side of the cage, slowly opening and closing its wings. Theo wrote one word in his notebook in giant letters: "WINGS!"$q$, 'draft')
