-- Passage rg4-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0c6de2ea-7037-5834-b4e1-e6f11c73135f', 'rg4-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How Maple Syrup Is Made$q$, $q$Maple syrup starts inside a tree. In late winter, sugar maple trees store sugar in their roots. When the weather warms up during the day but still freezes at night, something special happens. The changing temperature pushes sap up and down inside the tree.

This is when farmers tap the trees. A farmer drills a small hole in the trunk and puts in a spout. Sap drips out of the spout into a bucket or a long plastic tube. The sap looks like water and tastes only a little sweet.

Next, the sap must be boiled. Farmers pour it into a wide, flat pan over a hot fire. As the sap boils, the water in it turns to steam and floats away. The sugar stays behind. Slowly, the sap gets thicker, darker and sweeter.

Boiling takes a long time because sap is mostly water. It takes about forty gallons of sap to make just one gallon of syrup. That is why real maple syrup costs more than many other syrups.

The tapping season is short. Once nights stop freezing, the sap stops flowing well. When the buds on the trees open, the sap starts to taste bitter, so farmers pull out their spouts. The small holes heal, and the trees can be tapped again next year.$q$, 'draft')
