-- Passage rg4-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ba539979-1e90-5f1c-aefe-e0da4169252e', 'rg4-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Canal That Changed Millbrook$q$, $q$Two hundred years ago, the town of Millbrook was small and quiet. It sat in a valley between hills, far from any big city. Farmers there grew plenty of wheat and apples. But getting those crops to market was hard. Wagons bumped along muddy roads for days, and some of the fruit spoiled before it arrived.

In 1825, workers finished digging a canal. A canal is a waterway made by people. This one ran from Millbrook to a large river forty miles away. Mules walked on a path beside the water and pulled flat boats with ropes.

The canal changed life in Millbrook quickly. One boat could carry as much as twenty wagons. The trip took one day instead of four. Farmers could now sell more crops in the city, and they earned more money.

As trade grew, so did the town. New families moved to Millbrook to work on the boats. Shops, an inn and a school opened along the canal. Within ten years, the number of people in town had doubled.

Then, about fifty years later, a railroad reached the valley. Trains were even faster than canal boats, and they kept running in winter when the canal froze. Fewer boats used the canal each year. Today, the old mule path is a walking trail, and visitors can still see where the boats once passed.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
