-- Passage rg4-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7883d06a-43bc-50ce-a96b-b91f6a5303b1', 'rg4-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Leaves Change Color$q$, $q$In the fall, many trees turn bright red, orange and yellow. Have you ever wondered why? The answer has to do with how leaves make food.

During spring and summer, leaves are full of a green material called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll helps leaves use sunlight to make food for the tree. There is so much green that it hides other colors in the leaf.

Leaves also hold yellow and orange colors all summer long. You just cannot see them yet. When fall comes, the days get shorter and cooler. With less sunlight, trees stop making as much food. The chlorophyll breaks down, and the green slowly fades. Now the yellow and orange colors that were hiding can finally show.

Red is different. Red colors are not in the leaf all summer. Some trees make red color in the fall, when sugar gets trapped in their leaves. Sunny days and cool nights help trees make more red. That is why some years have brighter red leaves than others.

Finally, the tree seals off each leaf where it joins the branch. The leaf dries out and falls to the ground. Without its leaves, the tree can rest through the winter and save water until spring.$q$, 'draft')
