   $q$Why do students wedge the clay before shaping it?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To push out air bubbles that could crack the pot in the kiln","is_correct":true},{"text":"To mix the glaze into the clay so the bowl will be shiny","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-sequence"},{"text":"To help the clay dry out faster before it goes on the wheel","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To make the clay spin more quickly on the potter's wheel","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To push out air bubbles that could crack the pot in the kiln$q$, $q$Paragraph 2 says wedging "pushes out tiny bubbles of air," and trapped air "can expand in the hot kiln and crack the pot." Glaze is added much later, after the first firing.$q$, 'draft', 'f620bbae-f9c6-5c53-bd40-72fd4f3b3070')
