-- Passage rg4-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('67d81698-f3b7-522e-8f63-c0bc48d82de3', 'rg4-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Snowflake Forms$q$, $q$Every snowflake begins high in a cloud. The air up there is far below freezing. It is full of water vapor, which is water in the form of a gas you cannot see.

A snowflake needs a tiny starting point. Usually this is a speck of dust or pollen floating in the cloud. When water vapor touches the cold speck, it freezes and becomes a small ice crystal. Water freezes into a shape with six sides, so the crystal has six sides too.

As the crystal drifts through the cloud, more water vapor freezes onto it. New ice grows outward from its six corners. These become the arms of the snowflake. Because all six arms move through the same air at the same time, they usually grow in a similar pattern.

The temperature and wetness of the air shape each flake. In some conditions, flakes grow into flat plates. In others, they grow long, lacy arms. Each crystal takes its own path through the cloud, so no two follow exactly the same route.

When the crystal grows heavy enough, it falls. If the air near the ground stays cold, it lands as snow. If the air near the ground is warm, the flake melts on the way down and reaches the ground as rain.$q$, 'draft')
