-- Passage rg4-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('094e5117-61b9-5e8b-9c57-74e8d1793c3c', 'rg4-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Riders of the Pony Express$q$, $q$In 1860, sending a letter across the United States was slow. A letter could take weeks or even months to travel from Missouri to California. A group of business owners had a bold idea to speed things up. They started the Pony Express.

The Pony Express was a relay, a system where one person carries something part of the way and then hands it to the next person. Each rider carried the mail for one stretch of the trip. Riders also changed horses at stations about every ten to fifteen miles. That way, the horses never got too tired to gallop.

The riders had to be light, brave, and sturdy. Strong, tough riders could stay in the saddle for hours through rain, snow, and heat. They carried the mail in a mochila, which was a leather cover that fit over the saddle. A rider could swap it onto a fresh horse in just seconds.

With this system, mail crossed the country in about ten days. That was remarkable for the time, and people were amazed.

Yet the Pony Express lasted only about eighteen months. In 1861, workers finished a telegraph line that could send messages across the country in minutes. After that, the Pony Express was obsolete. Nobody needed riders on horses when a message could zip along a wire.$q$, 'draft')
