-- Passage rg4-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b05c6697-1ada-507d-b260-bd27db6076aa', 'rg4-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How Bridges Stay Up$q$, $q$Every day, bridges carry cars, trucks, and people over rivers and roads. How does a bridge hold so much weight without falling down?

The answer is that a good bridge spreads out its load. The load is all the weight a bridge must carry, from heavy trucks to the bridge's own steel and concrete. If all that weight pressed on one small spot, the bridge could sag or even collapse into the river below. Engineers, the people who plan and design bridges and buildings, work hard so that never happens.

One strong shape is the arch. An arch curves up like a rainbow. When weight presses down on top, the curve pushes the force out to each end and down into the ground. Some stone arch bridges have stood for hundreds of years.

Another kind is the suspension bridge. Its road hangs from thick steel cables. The cables stretch between tall towers and are held by heavy anchors in the ground at both ends. These bridges can span very long distances, reaching all the way across wide bays.

Engineers also test their ideas before building. They make small models and add weight until the models bend. These tests reveal weak spots that are hard to see just by looking. Then the engineers fix the problems before a real bridge is built.$q$, 'draft')
