-- Passage rg4-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b8d8f3f4-e7ab-57cb-b488-c6365d36ab3b', 'rg4-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Bats That Hunt With Sound$q$, $q$When the sun goes down, many bats leave their roosts to hunt. A roost is a place where bats rest, such as a cave, a hollow tree, or the space under a bridge. Most of these bats eat insects, and they catch them in the dark.

How do bats find tiny bugs at night? Many use a method called echolocation. A bat makes high squeaks as it flies. Each sound bounces off objects and comes back to the bat as an echo. By listening to the echoes, the bat can tell where a moth is, how big it is, and which way it is moving.

Bat squeaks are very high. Most are too high for people to hear, so the night may seem silent even when bats are calling all around us.

Bats are also very agile flyers. They can twist, dive, and turn in a split second to snatch a moth out of the air. A slow, clumsy flyer would never catch such quick prey. Moths and other insects are what bats hunt and eat.

Bats help people, too. One small bat can eat hundreds of insects in a single night. That means fewer mosquitoes biting people and fewer bugs eating farmers' crops.$q$, 'draft')
