   $q$In paragraph 5, the coach says, "You were a turtle at first, but now you are a swan." What does this metaphor mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Lena was fast at first but is now getting tired.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"Lena skated near the pond where swans swim.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Lena was scared of animals but now she likes them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Lena began slow and clumsy but now skates smoothly.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Lena began slow and clumsy but now skates smoothly.$q$, $q$A turtle moves slowly and a swan glides gracefully, and the passage says Lena can now go all the way around the rink "without falling." The metaphor shows how she improved, not that she slowed down.$q$, 'draft', '471a7fff-920b-5db7-a545-36572ab784df')
