-- Passage rg4-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('87396c19-503b-5a7b-85e4-5d7d977e21d3', 'rg4-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Life in the Desert$q$, $q$A desert is a place that gets very little rain. During the day, the sun beats down like a hot oven. At night, the air can turn cold. Plants and animals that live in deserts have special ways to survive.

Take the cactus. A cactus stores water inside its thick stem. After a rare rainstorm, the cactus drinks up water like a sponge. Then it uses that water slowly for weeks or even months. Its sharp spines keep thirsty animals from taking a bite.

Camels are also built for desert life. A camel's hump is a backpack full of fat. When food is hard to find, the camel's body can use the fat for energy. Camels have long eyelashes that guard their eyes against blowing sand. Their wide feet keep them from sinking into the soft ground.

Many small desert animals, like kangaroo rats, avoid the heat. They stay in cool burrows during the day and come out at night. When the sun goes down, the quiet desert comes alive.

Deserts may look empty at first glance. But they are full of life if you know where to look. Each plant and animal has found a clever way to make the most of very little.$q$, 'draft')
