   $q$The narrator tells what the hummingbird thinks but never tells what the heron is thinking while he stands still. How does this affect the story?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Readers know from the start that the heron is hunting, so nothing is a surprise.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-pov-effect"},{"text":"Like the hummingbird, readers do not know why he waits until he catches the fish.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Readers learn that the heron is angry at the hummingbird for laughing at him.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Readers cannot tell which bird is speaking when the dialogue is in quotes.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-dialogue-with-narration"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Like the hummingbird, readers do not know why he waits until he catches the fish.$q$, $q$Because the heron's thoughts are hidden, readers share the hummingbird's confusion until his neck "shot down into the water," which makes the catch a surprise. Nothing tells readers early that he is hunting.$q$, 'draft', '60431ae8-50d8-5029-856d-eb8f1056461c')
