insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('01988777-af1c-508b-9fd4-593549b6fc93', 'rg4-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Hot Air Balloon Rises$q$, $q$A hot air balloon can float high above fields and towns. It has no engine or wings. Instead, it rises because of one simple fact: hot air is lighter than cool air.

A hot air balloon has three main parts. The big cloth bag on top is called the envelope. Under it hangs a basket where the pilot and riders stand. Between them is a burner that makes a strong flame.

When the pilot turns on the burner, the flame heats the air inside the envelope. As the air warms, it spreads out and becomes lighter. Soon the warm air inside is lighter than the cool air outside. That difference lifts the whole balloon off the ground.

To go down, the pilot lets the air inside cool off. The pilot can also open a small flap at the top to let some hot air escape. Then the balloon sinks slowly back toward the earth.

A pilot cannot steer a balloon the way a driver steers a car. The wind decides which way it goes. Pilots learn that winds blow in different directions at different heights. By rising or sinking, they can catch a wind that moves the right way.$q$, 'draft')
